The goal of the model was to test another parameterization I've seen in paper. They purposefully have one prior with a spiked dsitribution and another that isn't. The idea is to choose between the priors.

Specifically, it either allows two parameters to be essentially equivalent (spiked prior) or to be different (non-spiked prior).

I couldn't find an easy way of speeding it up because ultimately the posterior will have a spike somewhere, so I think I'm giving up on it. Ran a little longer than my current method and provided less accurate inferences.

On Friday, December 9, 2016 at 3:15:56 PM UTC-6, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
> > On Dec 8, 2016, at 8:18 PM, Stephen Martin <hwki...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > ...
> >
> > target += log_sum_exp(log1m(pi) + normal_lpdf(alpha | beta, .01) , log(pi) + normal_lpdf(alpha | beta, 1);
> >
> > Is there a simple way of doing the above, but doing so on a unit scale? Intuitively, I thought I could do ... normal_lpdf(alpha*100 | beta, 1), but it leads to a very different estimate altogether for reasons I don't understand.
>
> Because it's not the same density.  Just write out the log density
> up to a propirtion:  look at the kernel:
>
>   0.5 * ((alpha * 100 - mu) / 1)^2 != 0.5 * ((alpha - mu) / .01)^2
>
> We typically recommend the Cauchy distribution if you want to concentrate
> most of the mass around a point but allow for long tails.  It's got
> better continuity and differentiability properties than this kind of
> mixture.
>
> - Bob
>

From u743bbacc59f4b25f at groups.google.com  Sat Dec 10 19:57:30 2016
From: u743bbacc59f4b25f at groups.google.com (Ben Goodrich)
Date: Sat, 10 Dec 2016 19:57:30 +0000
Subject: Re: Vectorising looping through an adjacency matrix for speed-up
Message-ID: <TyWX1o80oYU.eZQAGEx5CgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <TyWX1o80oYU.Xq-iji12CgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <TyWX1o80oYU.Xq-iji12CgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/TyWX1o80oYU/m/eZQAGEx5CgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On Saturday, December 10, 2016 at 2:00:20 PM UTC-5, Florian Klimm wrote:
> I am using Stan for statistical interference on networks. This works well but is fairly slow. Main reason is that I have to loop through all matrix elements (in fact only the upper triangular part). This process is not vectorised and I assume it would be much faster if I could transform my problem so that this would be possible.

That is not quite a correct assumption. The slowness is (in part) due to having sampling statements in the loop, which trigger a lot of memory allocations. Do this instead:

data {
  int<lower=1> n;
  int A[n,n];
}
transformed data {
  int n_choose_2;
  int A_lower[n * (n - 1) / 2]; // ignore integer division warnings
  n_choose_2 = n * (n - 1) / 2;
  {
    int pos;
    pos = 1;
    for (i in 1:n) for (j in (i + 1):n) {
      A_lower[pos] = A[i,j];
      pos = pos + 1;
    }
  }
}
model {
  vector[n_choose_2] lambda;
  int pos;
  pos = 1;
  for (i in 1:n) for (j in (i + 1):n) {
    lambda[pos] = p0 + p1 * exp(-p2 * dot_self(x[i] - x[j]));
    pos = pos + 1;
  }
  A_lower ~ poisson(lambda);
}

You would also probably be better off if you could change things around to use the poisson_log PMF instead of poisson.

Ben

