> In this case, in the continuous world Stan doesn’t force me to exhaustively sample all of continuous parameter space (something that can’t be done anyway, obviously!); by design, it spends most of its time in the high-probability regions of parameter space, and nearly none of its time where the probability is very very low. I would like to have the same capability in discrete space: hop around between discrete states, and spend most of its time where the probability is high

So would a huge number of scientists, statisticians, and
mathematicians.  We just don’t have any idea how to do
that for discrete spaces yet.  We’ve only recently figured
out how to do that for continuous spaces using gradient
information, but the correct generalization of “gradient”
information to discrete spaces is not know.  This is why
we try to figure out ways of marginalizing down to
continuous spaces and using the state of the art methods.

