I've got a model running that involves a truncated (gamma) distribution. I'd like to implement a posterior predictive check and that means simulating replicated data which respects these truncation limits. Is there an easy and efficient way to implement this?

My thought was to use a while statement to reject any samples outside of the truncation limit, but I'm not sure of how efficient this would be or whether this might introduce some bias in my replicated data.

For example one could try something like this:

generated quantities{
  real<lower = 0> y_rep[N]; //gamma distributed random variable with upper truncation limit U

  for(n in 1:N){
    int indic;
    real new_obs;

    indic = 0;
    while (indic = 0){
      new_obs = gamma_rng(alpha, beta);
      if (new_obs < U)
        indic = 1;
    }
    y_rep[n] = new_obs;
  }
}

Are there problems with this approach? Is there a better way?

