On Wednesday, December 14, 2016 at 5:18:23 PM UTC+2, martin.s...@gmail.com wrote:
> I understand you cannot have an automatic solution for everyone. I think you've come a long way though. Still, for a non-statistician it is, as you say, sometimes a pain to try to follow (and understand) all recommendations etc. regarding statistical analyses. After all, I/we really want to do proper statistics such that we can make the correct inferences for the questions we try to study.

I fully agree that it's difficult to follow the recommendations as they keep changing. I've noticed that big reason why our prior recommendations are changing is that Stan makes it so much easier to use better priors, but also the better inference and diagnostic methods in Stan help reveal problems in the old recommendations! We learn all the time and it takes some time to collect experiences to make new recommendations and thus sometimes the recommendations have lot of speculation, too. Lot of prior discussions have been collected to wiki https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Prior-Choice-Recommendations and a paper is in progress.

Aki

