Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/CfNtrttjlXA/m/FceHkcVhDQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You can---you only need the below-diagonal elements.  The
above diagonals are just negations of the below diagonal
elements and the diagonal is zero by construction.
You'd have to do the Pr[mu_diff[k,j] > 0 | data] outside.
If you instead define the integer indicators, the posterior
mean does that for you.  But sometimes we're actually interested
in the difference.

- Bob

> On Dec 20, 2016, at 2:15 AM, Stephen Martin <hwki...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Could you also make a parameter matrix that is just like:
>
> matrix[K,K] mu_diff;
> for(k in 1:K){
>   for(j in 1:K){
>     mu_diff[k,j] = mu[k] - mu[j];
>   }
> }
>
> Then look at p(mu_diff|D) > 0 ?
>
> On Monday, December 19, 2016 at 11:16:28 PM UTC-6, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
> > On Dec 20, 2016, at 12:10 AM, stefano <mangiol...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> >
> > 2016-12-20 15:52 GMT+11:00 Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com>:
> > So mu is a parameter 3-vector, sigma is a scalar, y[n] is a known scalar,
> > and the a[n, ] is a known simplex?
> >
> > Exactly
> >
> >
> > And you want to ask a question about the marginal posterior
> >
> >   p(mu | y, a)
> >
> > such as do the mu[1], mu[2] and mu[3] components have the same
> > posterior distribution?
> >
> > How do you want to measure difference?  You can look at one-sided
> > event probabilities like
> >
> >   Pr[mu[1] > mu[2] | y, a]
> >
> > Presumably if that's a very high value, you know they're different.
> >
> > This could work. But could this be integrated into the model or is something I would do after model execution?
> >
>
> generated quantities {
>    int<lower=0, upper=1> mu1_gt_mu2 = mu1 > mu2;
>    int<lower=0, upper=1> mu2_gt_mu3 = mu2 > mu3;
>    int<lower=0, upper=1> mu1_gt_mu3 = mu1 > mu3
> }
>
> Then the posterior mean will be the event probabilities.  Event probabilites
> are expectations of indicator variables like the above.
>
> If there are thousands, this approach won't work.
>
> - Bob
