Hi Allen,

Thank you for your reply. Unfortunately even implementing your suggestion I had the same error message.

After another search in the group I found this reply <https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/k7iRYWoZ3bA/TUzsxcn0AwAJ> from Bob Carpenter which lead me to a working solution. I've created a vector containing the starting point at which I need to slice 'y' (the vector containing all outcomes). In addition to that I had to implement a for-loop in the 'model' paragraph. I'm not sure how efficient this solution is but considering the small sample size it should do the job.

coins_code = """
data {
    int<lower=0> S;                      // # coins
    int<lower=0> N;                      // # tosses
    int<lower=0, upper=1> y[N];          // outcomes (must be sliced to obtain observations for a particular coin)
    int<lower=1, upper=N+1> start[S+1];  // starting indexes for slicing vector 'outcomes'

    int<lower=0> A;                      // alpha parameter for omega
    int<lower=0> B;                      // beta parameter for omega

    int<lower=2> KAPPA;                  // kappa = alpha + beta; concentration; dependence between omega and theta

}
parameters {
    real<lower=0, upper=1> theta[S];  // coin bias
    real<lower=0, upper=1> omega;     // mint bias
}
transformed parameters {
    real<lower=0> alpha;  // alpha parameter for prior on coin bias
    real<lower=0> beta;   // beta parameter for prior on coin bias

    alpha = omega*(KAPPA-2)+1;
    beta = (1-omega)*(KAPPA-2)+1;
}
model {
    omega ~ beta(A, B);
    theta ~ beta(alpha, beta);

    for (s in 1:S) {
        y[start[s]:start[s+1]-1] ~ bernoulli(theta[s]);
    }
}
"""

# generate observations for coin a and coin b
coin_a = [list(np.repeat(1, 3)), list(np.repeat(0, 12))]
obs_a = [item for sublist in coin_a for item in sublist]
coin_b = [list(np.repeat(1, 4)), list(np.repeat(0, 1))]
obs_b = [item for sublist in coin_b for item in sublist]
obs = [obs_a, obs_b]
obs_all = [item for sublist in obs for item in sublist]
obs_all

coins_data = {
    'S': 2,
    'N': len(obs_all),
    'y': obs_all,
    'start': [1, len(obs_a) + 1, len(obs_all) + 1],

    'A': 2,
    'B': 2,
    'KAPPA': 100
}

fit2 = pystan.stan(model_code=coins_code, data=coins_data,
                   iter=10000, chains=4)

print(fit2)
fit2.plot()
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

Please let me know if you see any way to optimise this code or something off.

Many thanks,
Gianluca

