One final wrap-up post on this topic:
I added Ben G's bounding function to the original stan file <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est_long.stan> where the data is in long format.
Another of Ben's suggestions was separate the 2 and 3 dimensional vectors in R before passing them into Stan. As can be seen here <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est_wide.stan>, the model block is much cleaner.
Finally, this last stan file <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est_sem.stan> goes after the covariance parameters indirectly, via latent variable modeling. Aside from the looping (which I know is making things slow), I think this is the way to go; extending Ben's correlation bounds to higher dimensions apparently involves solving for roots of higher order polynomials <http://www.math.nthu.edu.tw/~amen/2008/070830-1.pdf>.
The driver program for the previous file is still a single R program <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est.R>.

It's probably too late to help my friend with his fantasy football game, but I learned a lot about bayesian modeling and Stan. Thanks for the help and advice!

Ben Ogorek

On Sunday, December 18, 2016 at 11:27:51 AM UTC-6, Ben Ogorek wrote:
> Hi,
>
> I recently ran into a situation that suggested a "clustered" or vector regression model such that:
> The dimension of the random vector varies from cluster to cluster,
> The covariance matrices of the residuals are structured and depend on a fixed number of parameters.
> In existing software, I couldn't find any options that would allow for this, so I turned to Stan. I created a simple simulation in R <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est.R> where there is a structured covariance matrix for the vector (X1, X2, X3) such that:
> Var(X1) = Var(X2) = sigma_a^2,
> Var(X3) = sigma_b^2,
> Cov(X1, X2) = rho1,
> Cov(X1, X3) = rho2,
> Cov(X2, X3) = rho2,
> Where the clustered observations are observed as one of: (X1, X2), (X1, X3), (X2, X3), or (X1, X2, X3).
>
> In my Stan code <https://github.com/baogorek/Miscellaneous/blob/master/R_code/stan/ragged_array_cov_est.stan>, I believe I got it to work by creating a "master" covariance matrix in transformed parameters and then putting sub-segments of the data vector into local variable of fixed size in the model block. The only bad thing is having to "repeat myself" for every vector size:
>
>   for (i in 1:n_clusters) {
>     p = subj_upper[i] - subj_lower[i] + 1;
>     if (p == 2) {
